WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.629
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.929 --> 00:00:09.164
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.464 --> 00:00:17.457
Unit nineteen: Cybersecurity. In this unit, you will understand digital threats and how to defend against them.

4
00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:19.544
ransomware.

5
00:00:20.244 --> 00:00:25.088
ransomware means malicious software that locks files until a payment is made.

6
00:00:25.388 --> 00:00:27.631
The hospital was hit by ransomware.

7
00:00:28.331 --> 00:00:31.227
Your turn. Say: ransomware.

8
00:00:33.927 --> 00:00:35.114
ransomware.

9
00:00:36.117 --> 00:00:37.234
zero-day.

10
00:00:37.934 --> 00:00:42.546
zero-day means a software weakness unknown to its makers, with no fix yet.

11
00:00:42.846 --> 00:00:45.809
Hackers exploited a zero-day vulnerability.

12
00:00:46.509 --> 00:00:49.684
Your turn. Say: zero-day.

13
00:00:52.384 --> 00:00:53.373
zero-day.

14
00:00:54.383 --> 00:00:55.802
phishing campaign.

15
00:00:56.502 --> 00:01:02.693
phishing campaign means a large-scale attempt to trick people into revealing information.

16
00:01:02.993 --> 00:01:05.887
A phishing campaign targeted bank customers.

17
00:01:06.587 --> 00:01:09.715
Your turn. Say: phishing campaign.

18
00:01:12.415 --> 00:01:13.776
phishing campaign.

19
00:01:14.783 --> 00:01:15.692
firewall.

20
00:01:16.392 --> 00:01:20.098
firewall means a security system that controls network traffic.

21
00:01:20.398 --> 00:01:23.164
The firewall blocked thousands of attacks.

22
00:01:23.864 --> 00:01:26.887
Your turn. Say: firewall.

23
00:01:29.587 --> 00:01:30.414
firewall.

24
00:01:31.417 --> 00:01:32.836
penetration test.

25
00:01:33.536 --> 00:01:38.682
penetration test means an authorised simulated attack to find weaknesses.

26
00:01:38.982 --> 00:01:42.281
The penetration test revealed three serious flaws.

27
00:01:42.981 --> 00:01:46.296
Your turn. Say: penetration test.

28
00:01:48.996 --> 00:01:50.415
penetration test.

29
00:01:51.417 --> 00:01:52.615
threat actor.

30
00:01:53.315 --> 00:01:57.567
threat actor means a person or group responsible for a cyber attack.

31
00:01:57.867 --> 00:02:00.621
Security teams track known threat actors.

32
00:02:01.321 --> 00:02:04.333
Your turn. Say: threat actor.

33
00:02:07.033 --> 00:02:08.464
threat actor.

34
00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:10.695
state-sponsored.

35
00:02:11.395 --> 00:02:14.904
state-sponsored means supported or directed by a government.

36
00:02:15.204 --> 00:02:17.052
The attack was state-sponsored.

37
00:02:17.752 --> 00:02:20.799
Your turn. Say: state-sponsored.

38
00:02:23.499 --> 00:02:24.860
state-sponsored.

39
00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:26.874
data leak.

40
00:02:27.574 --> 00:02:31.606
data leak means the accidental release of private information.

41
00:02:31.906 --> 00:02:34.671
The data leak exposed millions of passwords.

42
00:02:35.371 --> 00:02:38.372
Your turn. Say: data leak.

43
00:02:41.072 --> 00:02:42.096
data leak.

44
00:02:43.083 --> 00:02:45.396
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:45.696 --> 00:02:49.577
Rithy, did you hear about the ransomware attack on the clinic?

46
00:02:50.883 --> 00:02:55.091
Yes. Apparently, the hackers got in through a phishing campaign.

47
00:02:56.383 --> 00:03:00.742
Typical. The weakest link is often human, not technical.

48
00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:04.154
Couldn't a firewall have stopped it?

49
00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:10.053
Not if someone opens the door from inside. It's a cat-and-mouse game.

50
00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:14.336
Some experts think it was state-sponsored.

51
00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:20.578
Possibly. Either way, they should have done a penetration test long ago.

52
00:03:21.883 --> 00:03:26.370
Better safe than sorry. I'm changing all my passwords tonight.

53
00:03:27.683 --> 00:03:30.658
Grammar focus: Idiomatic language.

54
00:03:30.958 --> 00:03:34.676
Idiomatic language makes your speech natural and expressive.

55
00:03:34.976 --> 00:03:41.724
The weakest link is the weakest part of a system; a cat-and-mouse game is a constant chase.

56
00:03:42.024 --> 00:03:45.115
Better safe than sorry means it's wise to be careful.

57
00:03:45.415 --> 00:03:50.654
Idioms make speech vivid, but avoid them in very formal writing such as reports.

58
00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:54.581
Now read and listen. The Invisible War.

59
00:03:54.881 --> 00:04:00.352
Every day, millions of cyber attacks target governments, companies and individuals.

60
00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:07.237
Ransomware can shut down hospitals, factories and even city services.

61
00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:13.243
Many attacks begin with a simple phishing campaign that tricks one employee.

62
00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:19.069
Some threat actors are criminal gangs, while others are state-sponsored groups.

63
00:04:20.383 --> 00:04:25.750
Security experts describe their work as a cat-and-mouse game with attackers.

64
00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:31.917
Penetration tests help organisations find weaknesses before criminals do.

65
00:04:33.217 --> 00:04:38.200
Even so, a single data leak can damage a company's reputation for years.

66
00:04:39.483 --> 00:04:43.596
In cybersecurity, as in life, prevention is better than cure.

67
00:04:44.883 --> 00:04:47.431
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:04:47.731 --> 00:04:51.426
Software that locks files until a payment is made is blank.

69
00:04:51.726 --> 00:04:56.064
A, ransomware. B, firewall. C, zero-day.

70
00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:02.363
The answer is A: ransomware.

71
00:05:03.850 --> 00:05:06.709
Humans are often the weakest blank in security.

72
00:05:07.009 --> 00:05:10.883
A, chain. B, link. C, point.

73
00:05:14.883 --> 00:05:16.906
The answer is B: link.

74
00:05:18.417 --> 00:05:20.094
'Better safe than blank.'

75
00:05:20.394 --> 00:05:23.397
A, sorry. B, sad. C, late.

76
00:05:27.383 --> 00:05:29.116
The answer is A: sorry.

77
00:05:30.617 --> 00:05:34.706
An authorised simulated attack to find weaknesses is a blank.

78
00:05:35.006 --> 00:05:41.005
A, data leak. B, penetration test. C, phishing campaign.

79
00:05:45.017 --> 00:05:48.444
The answer is B: penetration test.

80
00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:53.389
According to the text, how do many attacks begin?

81
00:05:53.689 --> 00:05:59.270
A, with a firewall. B, with a phishing campaign. C, with a penetration test.

82
00:06:03.283 --> 00:06:05.921
The answer is B: with a phishing campaign.

83
00:06:07.417 --> 00:06:12.983
Now it's your turn. Part one: How can ordinary people protect themselves from cybercrime?

84
00:06:18.283 --> 00:06:25.426
Part two: Use at least one idiom, give two practical tips and explain why they matter.

85
00:06:31.726 --> 00:06:42.215
Better safe than sorry: I always use two-step verification and never click unknown links. These matter because people are usually the weakest link, not the technology.

86
00:06:42.515 --> 00:06:44.793
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:06:45.783 --> 00:06:50.700
Great work today! You learned eight new words and idiomatic language.

88
00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:56.404
Today's value is online safety. Keep your password secret and be kind online.

89
00:06:56.704 --> 00:07:00.180
See you in Unit twenty. Goodbye!

90
00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:03.597
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

